  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\u27e83,-4,2\u27e9-\u27e81,-2,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82,-2,-1\u27e9 \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\u27e84,3,2\u27e9-\u27e81,-2,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,5,-1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=\u27e81,-2,3\u27e9$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ r & =\u27e81,-2,3\u27e9+s\u27e82,-2,-1\u27e9+t\u27e83,5,-1\u27e9,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
